{"term":"قضيب","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"qaḍīb","category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":15,"technical":85,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The standard clinical and Classical Arabic term for the penis, used in medical texts, formal literature, and Islamic jurisprudence.","english_term":"penis","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Root meaning 'rod' or 'stick'; attested in Classical Arabic medical literature including Ibn Sina's Canon of Medicine and in classical erotic poetry; the dominant clinical term across MSA."}
{"term":"عضو ذكري","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"ʿuḍw dhakarī","category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":10,"technical":75,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Formal compound phrase meaning 'male organ/member,' used as a clinical circumlocution for the penis in medical education and health communications.","english_term":"male organ / male member","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"A descriptive phrase rather than a lexicalized single word; ʿuḍw = organ/limb, dhakarī = male/masculine; preferred in formal written contexts where the directness of qaḍīb is felt to be immodest."}
{"term":"ذكر","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"dhakar","category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":10,"technical":60,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Primarily 'male' or 'masculine,' but used in Classical Arabic and some modern medical contexts as a synonym for the penis.","english_term":"penis (by extension from 'male')","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Highly polysemous: primary meanings are 'male,' 'memory/remembrance,' and 'mention'; the penile usage is context-dependent and more Classical than modern clinical; the related form dhukūra = maleness/virility. Appears in Quranic contexts without sexual connotation."}
{"term":"الحشفة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"al-ḥishfa","category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"glans","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":20,"technical":95,"recognition_pct_current":42,"recognition_pct_peak":42,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The clinical Arabic term for the glans penis, the rounded distal tip of the penis.","english_term":"glans penis","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Specialized anatomical term; lower recognition because it refers to a specific penile substructure; also culturally salient in the context of khitān (male circumcision), which exposes the ḥishfa."}
{"term":"القلفة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"al-qulfa","category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"foreskin","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":15,"technical":90,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The clinical Arabic term for the foreskin (prepuce), the retractable fold of skin covering the glans penis in uncircumcised males.","english_term":"foreskin / prepuce","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Higher recognition than most specialized penile anatomy terms because of widespread discussion of male circumcision (khitān) in Islamic religious and cultural discourse; the qulfa is explicitly what is removed in the circumcision rite."}
{"term":"الجسم الكهفي","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"al-jism al-kahfī","category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"corpus_cavernosum","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":99,"recognition_pct_current":18,"recognition_pct_peak":18,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The clinical Arabic translation of corpus cavernosum, the paired erectile tissue columns in the penis that engorge with blood during arousal.","english_term":"corpus cavernosum","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Purely medical/anatomical; jism = body/substance, kahfī = cavernous/cave-like (from kahf = cave); known almost exclusively to medical professionals and anatomy students."}
{"term":"القناة الناسفة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"al-qanāt al-nāsifa","category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"vas_deferens","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":99,"recognition_pct_current":12,"recognition_pct_peak":12,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Clinical Arabic term for the vas deferens or ejaculatory duct; literally 'the blasting/ejecting canal,' referencing the forceful expulsion of semen.","english_term":"vas deferens / ejaculatory duct","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Nāsifa derives from nasafa = to blast or explode; the descriptor better fits the ejaculatory duct functionally, but the term is used loosely across the seminal transport pathway; known almost exclusively to urologists and reproductive medicine specialists. Not the most standardized MSA term; alternatives include al-qanāt al-manawyya."}
{"term":"العضو التناسلي الذكري","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"al-ʿuḍw al-tanāsulī al-dhakarī","category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":80,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Full formal clinical phrase meaning 'the male reproductive organ,' the most circumspect clinical designation for the penis in medical education and public health materials.","english_term":"male reproductive organ / male genital organ","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"A phrase rather than a single lexeme; ʿuḍw = organ, tanāsulī = reproductive, dhakarī = male; preferred when clinical precision and social modesty must coexist, such as in sex-education curricula or governmental health publications."}
{"term":"أير","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"ayr","category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":96,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The most common and most offensive crude Arabic slang for the penis, equivalent in vulgarity to the most extreme English equivalents.","english_term":"cock / prick","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Extremely widespread across all Arabic-speaking regions; among the most taboo words in Arabic; appears in Classical erotic literature including Alf Layla wa-Layla; frequently used in insults, invective, and crude humor; the root may be related to a verb meaning 'to swing' or 'to go back and forth.'"}
{"term":"زب","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"zubb","category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":90,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Very common crude Arabic slang for the penis, slightly less extreme in vulgarity than 'ayr' but still highly offensive in formal contexts.","english_term":"dick / cock","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Used across virtually all Arabic-speaking regions; extremely high recognition; attested in Classical Arabic erotic poetry and prose; root may relate to a word meaning 'to exude' or 'to flow'; frequently used in insults, graffiti, and crude comedy."}
{"term":"زوبر","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"zūbar","category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":82,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":68,"peak_year":null,"regional":"EG","definition":"Colloquial crude slang for the penis, a phonologically augmented variant related to 'zubb'; somewhat more emphatic or playful in register.","english_term":"cock / dick","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Less universally distributed than 'zubb'; strongest presence in Egyptian and Levantine Arabic; the -ar suffix may function as an intensifier or augmentative in dialectal morphology."}
{"term":"خربوش","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"kharbūsh","category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":62,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":45,"recognition_pct_peak":45,"peak_year":null,"regional":"EG","definition":"A dialectal slang term for the penis, less vulgar than the core crude terms; primarily North African and Egyptian Arabic.","english_term":"cock / dick","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Root suggests something bushy, tangled, or unkempt; more playful and euphemistic than 'ayr' or 'zubb'; regional distribution limits pan-Arabic recognition; falls into a middle register between clinical and maximally crude."}
{"term":"ذيل","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"dhayl","category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":42,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'tail,' used as a widespread euphemistic slang for the penis based on the anatomical metaphor of a dangling appendage.","english_term":"tail (as penis euphemism)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primary meaning is the tail of an animal; the penile euphemism is a transparent shape-based metaphor understood broadly across Arabic dialects; parallel to English 'tail,' Spanish 'cola,' and French 'queue' used similarly."}
{"term":"بوظة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"būẓa","category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":52,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":48,"recognition_pct_peak":48,"peak_year":null,"regional":"EG","definition":"Primarily 'ice cream' in Egyptian and Levantine Arabic, secondarily used as crude slang for the penis, playing on the phallic shape of ice cream.","english_term":"cock / dick","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The double meaning enables deliberate ambiguity; the penile sense is not universally known and is primarily colloquial Egyptian; recognition as a penile term is substantially lower than the primary 'ice cream' meaning. Plausible deniability makes it a favored schoolyard term."}
{"term":"العصا","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"al-ʿaṣā","category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":35,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'the stick,' 'cane,' or 'staff,' used as a common and widely understood euphemism for the penis.","english_term":"stick / rod / staff (as penis euphemism)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Shares its root with the staff of Moses (ʿaṣā Mūsā) in the Quran, which creates incidental religious resonance; among the more socially tolerable penis euphemisms; parallel to English 'stick,' 'rod,' or 'staff' used similarly."}
{"term":"الخرطوم","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"al-khurṭūm","category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":38,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'the hose,' 'trunk,' or 'proboscis,' used as a colloquial euphemism for the penis based on its elongated shape.","english_term":"hose / trunk (as penis euphemism)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Also the Arabic name for Khartoum, capital of Sudan, which itself means 'elephant's trunk'; the penile euphemism is transparent; parallel to English 'garden hose' or 'trunk' as penile euphemisms; somewhat humorous in register."}
{"term":"عود","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"ʿūd","category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":30,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Primarily 'stick,' 'rod,' or 'wood,' and also the oud (lute) musical instrument; used as a euphemism for the penis based on the elongated wooden shape.","english_term":"rod / stick / wood (as penis euphemism)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Highly polysemous: ʿūd means wooden stick, the oud lute instrument, and aloes wood (used in perfumery); the penile euphemism capitalizes on the 'stick/rod' sense; English 'wood' as slang for erection offers a rough parallel; mildly humorous rather than genuinely crude."}
{"term":"جهاز","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"jihāz","category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":22,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'device,' 'apparatus,' or 'equipment'; used as a widespread polite euphemism for the penis or male genitalia generally.","english_term":"equipment / apparatus (as genital euphemism)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Common across Arabic-speaking contexts; the word also denotes household appliances and electronic devices, creating deliberate or incidental ambiguity; parallel to English 'equipment' or 'gear' as genital euphemisms; one of the softest-register items in this set."}
{"term":"مهبل","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"mahbal","category":"organs","subcategory":"vagina","concept":"vagina","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":15,"technical":90,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The standard clinical MSA term for the vagina, used in gynecological, medical, and anatomical contexts.","english_term":"vagina","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The primary medical term for the internal vaginal canal in Modern Standard Arabic; root relates to h-b-l (rope/cord) or possibly the sense of 'conduit'; used in medical texts, health education curricula, and gynecological practice across the Arab world."}
{"term":"فرج","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"farj","category":"organs","subcategory":"vagina","concept":"vulva","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"classical_specialized","crude":22,"technical":50,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Classical Arabic term for the female pudendum or vulva; also means 'opening,' 'gap,' or 'relief'; used in Quranic verses and Classical literature to denote the female genital region.","english_term":"vulva / female pudendum","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Highly polysemous: primary meanings include 'relief,' 'deliverance,' and 'gap/opening'; the genital meaning is understood by virtually all Arabic speakers; appears in Quranic verses enjoining chastity (ḥifẓ al-farj = guarding one's private parts); Classical erotic literature uses it freely. The word's spiritual register coexists awkwardly with its anatomical one."}
{"term":"الشفة الكبرى","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"al-shafa al-kubrā","category":"organs","subcategory":"vagina","concept":"labia_majora","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":10,"technical":95,"recognition_pct_current":38,"recognition_pct_peak":38,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The clinical Arabic term for the labia majora, the outer folds of skin forming the outer boundary of the vulva.","english_term":"labia majora","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Literally 'the great/major lip'; shafa = lip, kubrā = greater; purely clinical term known primarily to medical professionals, anatomy students, and educated speakers who have encountered gynecological education."}
{"term":"الشفة الصغرى","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"al-shafa al-ṣughrā","category":"organs","subcategory":"vagina","concept":"labia_minora","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":10,"technical":95,"recognition_pct_current":32,"recognition_pct_peak":32,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The clinical Arabic term for the labia minora, the inner folds of skin flanking the vaginal opening.","english_term":"labia minora","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Literally 'the small/minor lip'; shafa = lip, ṣughrā = smaller; even lower recognition than the labia majora term as a distinct named anatomical structure; known primarily to medical professionals."}
{"term":"دهليز المهبل","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"dahlīz al-mahbal","category":"organs","subcategory":"vagina","concept":"vulvar_vestibule","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":99,"recognition_pct_current":12,"recognition_pct_peak":12,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The clinical Arabic term for the vulvar vestibule, the anatomical space between the labia minora containing the vaginal and urethral openings.","english_term":"vulvar vestibule / vaginal vestibule","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Literally 'vestibule/corridor of the vagina'; dahlīz = entrance hall or vestibule; a highly specialized anatomical term known almost exclusively to gynecologists and pelvic floor specialists; effectively absent from lay vocabulary."}
{"term":"الغشاء العذري","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"al-ghishāʾ al-ʿadhrī","category":"organs","subcategory":"vagina","concept":"hymen","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":20,"technical":80,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The clinical Arabic term for the hymen; literally 'the virginal membrane,' reflecting both its anatomical nature and its profound cultural significance in Arabic-speaking societies.","english_term":"hymen","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Higher recognition than most specialized gynecological terms because of the enormous social weight placed on female virginity in many Arabic-speaking cultures; ghishāʾ = membrane/veil, ʿadhrī = of virginity (related to ʿadhrāʾ = virgin); the term is used in legal, religious, and medical discourse around honor crimes and marriage."}
{"term":"الأعضاء التناسلية الأنثوية","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"al-aʿḍāʾ al-tanāsuliyya al-unthawiyya","category":"organs","subcategory":"vagina","concept":"genitalia","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":85,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":68,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Full formal clinical phrase meaning 'the female reproductive organs,' the broadest and most circumspect clinical designation for female sexual anatomy.","english_term":"female genitalia / female reproductive organs","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"A descriptive phrase rather than a single lexeme; aʿḍāʾ = organs/members, tanāsuliyya = reproductive, unthawiyya = female; broader than mahbal (vagina) as it encompasses all internal and external female sexual anatomy; preferred in sex-education curricula and governmental health publications where maximal clinical distance is desired."}
